{"hash":"a951ee0008d401f9","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":87.044,"said":"You have climbed into the temple’s great hall, about fourteen feet high and carried on sixty massive pillars. Above you the temple rises through five storeys, each with its own shrine. Who built it, and when? The temple trust says honestly that no authentic record tells us. Notice something unusual: the old sanctum faces one way and the newer hall another, which is why the Jyotirlinga sits neither beneath the spire nor in line with the main door. The worship, though, tells a story of its own. Every day the morning puja is performed by the temple trust, the afternoon puja by priests of the Scindia family, and the evening puja by priests of the Holkar family, two old royal houses of this land, still serving the Lord each day. Near the entrance is the Govindeshwar cave. Here, tradition says, the young Shankara found his guru, Govinda Bhagavatpada, and received initiation. That young seeker became Adi Shankaracharya, and today the Statue of Oneness, one hundred and eight feet tall, shows him as a boy on Mandhata hill. Now climb with me to the upper storey. Someone you may already know is waiting there.\n\nDid you know? Pilgrims first visit the Panchmukhi Ganesh before the main temple; his five-faced image is believed to have emerged from the same rock as Omkareshwar.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1115},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1131,"end":1281}],"words":[[0.098,0.156,0,3],[0.266,0.168,4,8],[0.447,0.457,9,16],[0.915,0.228,17,21],[1.156,0.108,22,25],[1.276,0.517,26,34],[1.805,0.337,35,40],[2.153,0.421,41,45],[2.778,0.337,47,52],[3.127,0.457,53,61],[3.596,0.288,62,66],[3.896,0.325,67,71],[4.233,0.24,72,75],[4.485,0.361,76,83],[4.858,0.156,84,86],[5.026,0.421,87,92],[5.459,0.409,93,100],[5.879,0.481,101,108],[7.26,0.337,110,115],[7.608,0.12,116,119],[7.74,0.12,120,123],[7.873,0.409,124,130],[8.293,0.493,131,136],[8.798,0.156,137,144],[8.966,0.385,145,149],[9.36